1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:08,430
FORK is operations planning in the Business and Enterprise
track. It helps a coordinator test a plan before a disruption
makes the consequences real.

2
00:00:09,832 --> 00:00:19,966
This synthetic food bank has two hundred forty meals, two
hundred sixty four requested, and three delivery vehicles. The
coordinator can compare plans before dispatch.

3
00:00:21,365 --> 00:00:34,277
Three strategies use identical starting resources and different
priorities. We show ordinary greedy routing too. It initially
delivers two hundred twenty four meals, more than every named
policy. The tradeoffs stay visible.

4
00:00:35,678 --> 00:00:45,228
Now the same vehicle fails at the same time in every world.
Completed deliveries stay completed, failed cargo stays
stranded, and only the remaining plan can change.

5
00:00:46,630 --> 00:01:01,783
With this breakdown, every adapted plan delivers one hundred
sixty eight meals. Their fixed-plan controls deliver one hundred
forty four, one hundred twenty, and one hundred forty four.
Recovery is measured against the same shock, not an easier
scenario.

6
00:01:03,185 --> 00:01:15,198
A separate referee reconstructs movement, cargo, and timing,
then recomputes the outcomes. This verifies consistency inside
the declared model. It does not prove an optimal route or real-
world safety.

7
00:01:16,598 --> 00:01:29,563
A person reviews the evidence, approves the current revision,
and exports the simulated plan. No vehicle is dispatched. FORK
makes operational tradeoffs visible, with independent checks and
a human making the decision.
